Most of my time, about 80%, was on Azure landing zone projects. Usually, I’d get a scope of work with steps, and my first task was to write a spec document — basically a plan that explained how we’d do it and what technologies we’d use.

From there, I built the infrastructure with Terraform, using the CAF module, which covers everything from networking and firewall rules up to more advanced resources like databases, AKS clusters, storage accounts, and key vaults.

After the infra was ready, I also handled the application deployment on Kubernetes. I worked with Helm charts, ingress controllers, and services to get apps up and running.

On the CI/CD side, some companies already had Git, some didn’t. When we worked in GitHub, we managed all the Terraform code there and I built GitHub Actions pipelines. The pipelines had testing, security scans, linting, and steps for tagging and pushing images. For customers who needed it, I set up self-hosted runners so everything stayed in their private environment.

So basically my day-to-day was a mix of planning, building infra with Terraform, deploying on Kubernetes with Helm, and automating everything with GitHub Actions. It gave me a lot of hands-on practice with both the infra side and the DevOps automation side.”